course: dictionary
lesson: 6183
title: Mirror: one word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. «Toad catches sight of himself in the mirror, the rusty black bonnet perched over one eye.» Here a mirror is the glass you look into, because you see yourself. Ask: what would fit here? A shiny glass you can see yourself in fits.
explain: Mirror can also be a verb. To mirror something is to copy it or show it like a reflection. If a lake «mirrors» the sky, the water shows the sky back to you, just as a mirror would. Check by swapping in the meaning: does the sentence still make sense?
explain: In maths, a mirror line, also called a line of symmetry, splits a shape into two matching halves. A mirror image is a reflection where left and right are swapped. Each meaning comes from the same idea of showing something back to you.
fact: A mirror is a piece of special glass with a shiny back, so you can see yourself and things behind you.
fact: To mirror something is to copy it exactly or show it like a reflection.
fact: A mirror image is a reflection where left and right are swapped.
fact: Light bounces off a mirror at the same angle it hits it, which is how periscopes work.
fact: «If you cannot see the driver's face in the truck's mirror, the driver cannot see you either.»
exercise: «Toad catches sight of himself in the mirror.» What is a mirror here? | A window that opens | A shiny metal spoon | A kind of rusty bonnet | *A piece of special glass you can see yourself in
exercise: «The lake mirrored the trees.» What does mirrored mean? | Cut down | *Copied, showing them like a reflection | Moved far away | Chased
exercise: What is a mirror image? | Two shapes that are the same size | *A reflection where left and right are swapped | A photo taken in the dark | A picture of a sunset
test: In maths, what does a mirror line split a shape into? | One big piece | *Two matching halves | Four corners | Three uneven parts
test: «The driver of a truck cannot see a cyclist in its blind spots.» If you cannot see the driver's face in the truck's mirror, what is true? | The truck is turning left only | *The driver cannot see you either | The driver can see you | The mirror is broken
exam: Which sentence uses mirror as a verb? | The mirror above her bed was cracked | Mirrors are made of glass | I looked into the mirror | *The lake mirrored the sky
